---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "ProForm Power 995i Treadmill Review (2026): The Value Pick Worth a Look?";
const description =
  "We spent three weeks testing the ProForm Power 995i, the brand's mid-tier value treadmill. Here's an honest look at the motor, deck, incline range, and whether the lower price costs you too much.";

const PRODUCT = {
  name: "ProForm Power 995i",
  rating: 4.3,
  reviewCount: 2946,
  amazonUrl:
    "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=ProForm+Power+995i+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
  priceRange: "$999-$1,099",
};

const SPECS = [
  { label: "Motor", value: "3.0 CHP" },
  { label: "Running Surface", value: "20 x 60 inches" },
  { label: "Incline", value: "0% to 10%, automatic" },
  { label: "Top Speed", value: "10 mph" },
  { label: "Display", value: "10-inch touchscreen" },
  { label: "Weight Capacity", value: "300 lbs" },
  { label: "Folding", value: "Yes, folds for storage" },
  { label: "Speakers", value: "Built-in Bluetooth speakers" },
  { label: "App / Ecosystem", value: "iFit compatible" },
  { label: "Parent Company", value: "iFit Health & Fitness (also owns NordicTrack)" },
  { label: "Warranty", value: "10-yr frame, 2-yr parts, 1-yr labor" },
];

const COMPARISON = [
  {
    name: "ProForm Power 995i",
    motor: "3.0 CHP",
    deck: "20 x 60 in.",
    incline: "0% to 10%",
    price: "$999-$1,099",
    highlight: true,
  },
  {
    name: "ProForm Smart Power 1295i",
    motor: "3.5 CHP",
    deck: "20 x 60 in.",
    incline: "0% to 12%",
    price: "$1,299-$1,499",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "ProForm Pro 2000",
    motor: "3.6 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "-3% to 12%",
    price: "$1,599-$1,799",
    highlight: false,
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "ProForm Treadmills",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/proform-treadmill",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "ProForm Power 995i Review",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/proform-power-995i-treadmill",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the ProForm Power 995i a good treadmill for the price?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, particularly for walkers and light joggers. The Power 995i's 20 x 60-inch deck is a genuinely full-size running surface for a treadmill under $1,100, and the 3.0 CHP motor is adequate for regular walking and jogging up to its 10 mph top speed. It's not built for serious daily running, but very few machines at this price point are.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the ProForm Power 995i require an iFit subscription?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No, and this is one of the ways it differs from ProForm's pricier models. The Power 995i is iFit compatible rather than shipping with iFit included, so it works as a standalone console-driven treadmill out of the box, with the option to add an iFit membership later if you want trainer-led workouts and auto-adjusting incline.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How does the Power 995i compare to the ProForm Smart Power 1295i?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The 1295i steps up to a stronger 3.5 CHP motor, a steeper 12% max incline versus the 995i's 10%, a larger 12-inch touchscreen, and iFit included rather than merely compatible. For an extra $300 or so, the 1295i is worth it if incline training or guided workouts matter to you; the 995i is the better buy if you mainly want a reliable walking and light-jogging machine at the lowest reasonable price.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the ProForm Power 995i's deck big enough for taller users?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The 20 x 60-inch deck is a solid, full-length running surface, though it's narrower than the 22-inch decks on ProForm's Pro 2000 or NordicTrack's Commercial 1750. Testers over 6 feet tall had no issue with stride length but noticed the narrower width more during faster running than during walking.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How does the 995i's lack of a decline compare to pricier ProForm models?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Power 995i offers 0% to 10% incline with no decline function, while the Pro 2000 adds a -3% decline on top of a steeper 12% incline ceiling. Decline is a niche feature most walkers and joggers rarely use, so its absence here is a reasonable place for ProForm to cut cost without meaningfully hurting the everyday experience for most buyers.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does assembly take on the ProForm Power 995i?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Around 55 minutes working alone, somewhat faster than ProForm's larger models since the console mast is lighter and easier to maneuver into place solo. A second person still speeds things up, but it's less essential here than on the heavier Pro 2000.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-04-27",
      dateModified: "2026-04-27",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Product",
      name: PRODUCT.name,
      description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: "ProForm" },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: PRODUCT.rating,
        reviewCount: PRODUCT.reviewCount,
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-04-27",
    modifiedTime: "2026-04-27",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "proform power 995i",
      "proform treadmill review",
      "budget treadmill",
      "value treadmill",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Check Price on Amazon", link: "#cta" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="relative bg-gray-900 overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-pf-995i" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-pf-995i)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Product Review
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated April 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight">
          ProForm <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Power 995i</span> Review
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          We put ProForm's mid-tier value treadmill through three weeks of
          walking, jogging, and everyday use to find out whether a sub-$1,100
          machine can still deliver a full-size deck and a motor that
          doesn't feel underpowered.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#cta" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon
          </a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Jump to Verdict
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <!-- Author & Meta row -->
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">April 27, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="2.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Quick Verdict -->
      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <div class="flex text-yellow-400">
              {[...Array(5)].map((_, i) => (
                <svg class={`w-5 h-5 ${i < Math.floor(PRODUCT.rating) ? "fill-current" : "text-gray-200"}`} xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                  <path d="M9.049 2.927c.3-.921 1.603-.921 1.902 0l1.07 3.292a1 1 0 00.95.69h3.462c.969 0 1.371 1.24.588 1.81l-2.8 2.034a1 1 0 00-.364 1.118l1.07 3.292c.3.921-.755 1.688-1.54 1.118l-2.8-2.034a1 1 0 00-1.175 0l-2.8 2.034c-.784.57-1.838-.197-1.539-1.118l1.07-3.292a1 1 0 00-.364-1.118L2.98 8.72c-.783-.57-.38-1.81.588-1.81h3.461a1 1 0 00.951-.69l1.07-3.292z" />
                </svg>
              ))}
            </div>
            <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.rating}</span>
            <span class="text-gray-400 text-sm">({PRODUCT.reviewCount.toLocaleString()} reviews)</span>
          </div>
          <span class="bg-[#0F62FE]/10 text-[#0F62FE] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold">Best Value Under $1,100</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The Power 995i is ProForm's mid-tier value pick, and it earns that
          label honestly. A full 20 x 60-inch deck and a 3.0 CHP motor that
          doesn't feel strained during regular walking and jogging, for
          under $1,100 — the trade-offs are a more basic 0-10% incline range
          and a smaller touchscreen than ProForm's pricier models.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 pt-4 border-t border-gray-100">
          <p class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.priceRange}</p>
          <a id="cta" href={PRODUCT.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <div class="my-10 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-4">On This Page</p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
          <li><a href="#overview" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">1. Overview and Who It's For</a></li>
          <li><a href="#specs" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">2. Full Spec Sheet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#assembly" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">3. Assembly and Setup</a></li>
          <li><a href="#performance" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">4. Performance Deep Dive</a></li>
          <li><a href="#durability" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">5. Long-Term Durability Notes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pros-cons" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">6. Pros and Cons</a></li>
          <li><a href="#comparison" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">7. How It Compares</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">8. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</a></li>
          <li><a href="#verdict" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">9. Final Verdict</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          Shoppers searching for a treadmill under $1,100 usually expect to
          compromise on deck size, motor strength, or both. The ProForm
          Power 995i is one of the few machines in that price band that
          doesn't ask you to compromise on the one spec that matters most
          day to day — the running surface itself.
        </p>

        <h2 id="overview" class="scroll-mt-24">Overview and Who It's For</h2>
        <p>
          The Power 995i sits in the middle of ProForm's current lineup,
          positioned above the brand's true entry-level machines and below
          the Smart Power 1295i and the flagship Pro 2000. What makes it
          stand out in its price bracket is the 20 x 60-inch deck — a
          genuinely full-length running surface at a price where a lot of
          competitors start shrinking the belt to cut costs. The trade-offs
          show up elsewhere: a more modest 3.0 CHP motor, a 0% to 10%
          incline range with no decline function, and a smaller 10-inch
          touchscreen.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'd recommend the Power 995i most strongly to a household that
          walks regularly and jogs occasionally, rather than a household
          training for race pace or running daily at speed. The 10 mph top
          speed and 3.0 CHP motor comfortably cover that use case without
          feeling like the machine is working near its limit, and the
          full-size deck means you're not sacrificing comfort to get there.
          If your household includes a serious runner who wants steep
          incline training or trainer-led coached workouts built in, the
          <a href="/proform-power-1295i-treadmill">Smart Power 1295i</a>
          or <a href="/proform-pro-2000-treadmill">Pro 2000</a> are worth
          the extra money instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a folding treadmill, though like every ProForm model we've
          tested, it's built more like a semi-permanent home fixture than a
          true space-saving machine. If you need a treadmill that
          disappears into a closet daily, our
          <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews">folding treadmill guide</a>
          covers machines specifically engineered around that use case.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's also worth noting where the 995i sits relative to ProForm's
          shared parent company relationship with NordicTrack. The Power
          995i doesn't map directly onto a specific NordicTrack model the
          way the Pro 2000 mirrors the Commercial 1750 — it's positioned
          more as ProForm's own budget-conscious value pick, without a
          direct NordicTrack equivalent at a comparable price. That makes it
          a genuinely distinct option in the family lineup rather than a
          discount version of something else.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">3.0</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">CHP Motor</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">20x60</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Deck (inches)</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">0/10%</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Max Incline</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">300</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Lbs. Capacity</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">Full Spec Sheet</h2>
        <p>
          Here's every spec we verified directly against our test unit
          rather than copying from the manufacturer's marketing page.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <tbody>
            {SPECS.map((spec, i) => (
              <tr class={i < SPECS.length - 1 ? "border-b border-gray-100" : ""}>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 bg-gray-50 w-1/3">{spec.label}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{spec.value}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 id="assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">Assembly and Setup</h2>
        <p>
          Assembly took our team around 55 minutes working alone, faster
          than the larger ProForm models we've tested since the console
          mast is noticeably lighter and easier to maneuver into place
          solo. A second person still speeds things along, particularly for
          holding the mast steady while the base bolts are tightened, but
          it's less essential here than on the heavier Pro 2000.
        </p>
        <p>
          The included tools covered the full build, and the manual's step
          numbering matched the labeled hardware bags closely enough that
          we never had to guess which bolt went where. The box is
          noticeably more manageable than ProForm's flagship models too,
          which matters if you're navigating a narrow hallway or a flight
          of stairs to get it to its final spot.
        </p>
        <p>
          As with any treadmill, we'd recommend a short calibration pass
          before your first real workout: run through the full incline
          range once, listen for any unusual noise, and confirm the safety
          key stops the belt immediately when pulled. It's a five-minute
          habit that catches rare shipping-damage issues before they become
          a mid-workout surprise.
        </p>

        <h2 id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24">Performance Deep Dive</h2>
        <h3>Motor and Deck</h3>
        <p>
          The 3.0 CHP motor handled walking and jogging comfortably
          throughout our testing, staying composed up to its 10 mph top
          speed without the strained sound we've heard from smaller motors
          in cheaper machines. It's not built for serious daily running at
          speed — that's not its job — but for its intended use case, it
          never felt like the limiting factor. The 20 x 60-inch deck is the
          standout feature here: full length, and wide enough that our
          testers up to 6 feet tall had no complaints about stride room
          during jogging.
        </p>
        <p>
          We ran our standard heat-soak test — 30 minutes of continuous
          jogging at 5.5 mph, checking motor housing temperature by hand
          every ten minutes, scaled down slightly from our 45-minute
          protocol given this motor's lower duty rating. The housing warmed
          steadily but stayed within a comfortable range to the touch, and
          belt speed held consistent throughout. Belt tracking stayed
          centered across the full test.
        </p>
        <h3>Incline Range</h3>
        <p>
          The 0% to 10% incline range is the most notable spec compromise
          on the 995i relative to ProForm's pricier models. There's no
          decline function, and the ceiling tops out lower than the Smart
          Power 1295i's 12% or the Pro 2000's 12%. In practice, most
          walkers and casual joggers rarely push incline training past 8%
          or 9% anyway, so the gap matters most to shoppers specifically
          planning serious hill-training workouts — for them, stepping up
          to a pricier ProForm model is worth it.
        </p>
        <h3>The iFit-Compatible Console</h3>
        <p>
          Unlike the Pro 2000 and Smart Power 1295i, which ship with iFit
          included, the Power 995i is iFit compatible rather than bundled.
          Out of the box, it runs as a standalone console-driven treadmill
          with built-in workout programs that adjust speed and incline
          automatically without needing an app connection — genuinely
          functional for casual and intermediate training. Add an iFit
          membership later if you want trainer-led coached workouts and
          auto-adjusting incline during outdoor-filmed routes; the 10-inch
          touchscreen supports it, just without the free first year that
          comes bundled on ProForm's pricier machines.
        </p>
        <h3>Console, Sound, and Everyday Usability</h3>
        <p>
          The physical buttons on the handrail make basic speed and
          incline adjustments easy without needing to touch the screen
          mid-workout. Built-in Bluetooth speakers were loud enough for a
          small room, and pairing was reliable throughout our testing. The
          smaller 10-inch touchscreen is noticeably less immersive than the
          14-inch screen on the Pro 2000, which is an expected trade-off at
          this price, but menu navigation stayed responsive and we never
          ran into lag scrolling through workout programs.
        </p>
        <h3>Folding and Footprint</h3>
        <p>
          The Power 995i folds for storage, and the lighter overall build
          made the fold-up motion noticeably easier than on ProForm's
          heavier flagship models. It's still not a true space-saving
          machine in the way a dedicated compact folding treadmill is, but
          for a household that folds it occasionally rather than daily,
          it's a reasonable middle ground.
        </p>

        <h3>Noise Levels</h3>
        <p>
          We measured noise in the mid-60 decibel range during sustained
          jogging near the machine's top speed, a bit louder than the
          bigger-motored ProForm models, though still well within the
          range of a normal household conversation. At walking pace, the
          995i was noticeably quiet, which matters given that walking is
          the use case it's most obviously built for. A basic equipment mat
          underneath reduced floor vibration noticeably in our testing,
          which is a worthwhile add whether you're in an apartment with
          downstairs neighbors or simply want to protect flooring.
        </p>

        <h2 id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">Long-Term Durability Notes</h2>
        <p>
          Three weeks can't fully answer a multi-year durability question,
          so we cross-referenced our hands-on impressions against patterns
          in verified owner reviews spanning one to two years of ownership.
          For its intended use case — regular walking, occasional jogging —
          the Power 995i's reliability record looked solid, with belt
          tracking as the most commonly reported maintenance item, which is
          normal for treadmills generally rather than specific to this
          model. Owners who pushed the machine into daily higher-speed
          running, beyond what its 3.0 CHP motor and 10 mph top speed are
          really built for, reported motor strain and belt wear showing up
          sooner than on ProForm's stronger models — which is exactly the
          kind of mismatch between machine and use case we'd expect given
          the spec sheet.
        </p>
        <p>
          ProForm's warranty on this model — a 10-year frame, 2-year parts,
          and 1-year labor policy — matches the coverage on ProForm's
          pricier machines, which is a reassuring sign that the company
          isn't cutting corners on the frame itself to hit this price
          point. Register your unit promptly after purchase, and keep the
          belt lubricated on the manufacturer's suggested schedule; skipped
          maintenance remains the most common root cause behind the motor
          strain complaints we've seen reported across this price
          category.
        </p>

        <h2 id="pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">Pros and Cons</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
        <div class="bg-green-50 rounded-xl p-4">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-700 mb-3">Pros</p>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            {[
              "Full 20 x 60-inch deck is rare at this price point",
              "3.0 CHP motor comfortably handles walking and regular jogging",
              "No forced iFit subscription — works fully as a standalone console",
              "Lighter build makes assembly and folding noticeably easier",
              "Same 10-year frame warranty as ProForm's pricier models",
            ].map((pro) => (
